.TH SCANDIR 3 "October 13, 2026"
.UC 5
.SH NAME
scandir, alphasort \- scan a directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.B #include <sys/dir.h>
.PP
.B int scandir(const char *dirname, struct direct ***namelist,
.B "    int (*select_entry)(const struct direct *),"
.B "    int (*compar)(const struct direct **, const struct direct **));"
.PP
.B int alphasort(const struct direct **left, const struct direct **right);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I scandir
reads
.I dirname
and copies selected directory entries into separately allocated records.
On success, the function returns the entry count and stores the allocated
pointer array through
.IR namelist .
An empty selection returns zero and stores a null pointer.
.PP
The optional
.I select_entry
function receives a directory entry and returns nonzero to include the entry.
A null selector includes every entry returned by
.IR readdir (3).
The pointer array grows from the accepted-entry count.
Each copied record occupies
.B DIRSIZ(entry)
bytes, and its
.B d_reclen
reports that allocated length rather than the source directory-block span.
.PP
The optional
.I compar
function receives pointers to two const directory-entry pointers.
The function returns a negative, zero or positive value to order the first
entry before, with or after the second entry.
An iterative heap sort orders the pointer array with per-call comparator
state. Equal entries have unspecified relative order.
A null comparator preserves the directory's enumeration order.
.PP
.I alphasort
compares entry names with
.IR strcmp (3)
in the fixed bytewise locale and preserves
.IR errno .
The interface retains the historical
.B struct direct
name and header; locale-aware collation remains unavailable.
.PP
The caller releases a successful result by freeing each entry and then
freeing the pointer array with
.IR free (3).
On failure,
.I scandir
releases its allocated records and pointer array, closes the directory
stream, preserves the deciding error in
.IR errno ,
and leaves the caller's
.I namelist
value unchanged.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I scandir
returns \-1 for directory-open, metadata, read or allocation failure.
A descriptor referring to a nondirectory produces
.BR ENOTDIR .
An entry count exceeding the return type produces
.BR EOVERFLOW .
An unrepresentable allocation size produces
.BR ENOMEM .
.SH SEE ALSO
directory(3), malloc(3), strcmp(3), dir(5)
